<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">German General Staff</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as General Staff</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The German General Staff was the planning body of the Prussian and German army. Famous for its professionalism, it planned the wars of German unification and prepared the war plans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a> used in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/german-general-staff.jpg" alt="German General Staff" width="320" height="124" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Generalstabsgeb%C3%A4ude_(Berlin).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the 19th century Prussian victories depended largely on the sovereign's own skill, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> generalship declined, though a Quartermaster General Staff of adjutants and engineers survived him. In the last years of the 18th century reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-von-gneisenau">August von Gneisenau</a> had military experts assigned to assist the generals. The staff existed unofficially from 1806 and was established by law in 1814. On 15 October 1810 Scharnhorst opened the General War School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-von-clausewitz">Carl von Clausewitz</a> was among the first directors; his syllabus, drawn from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, became the staff's central doctrine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Officers were chosen for intelligence and proven merit rather than patronage or wealth, and their long training aimed to give a body of experts a common method, so that any of them could take over another's work. They alternated between line and staff duties but remained members for life. The staff itself was small, often fifty officers and rarely more than a hundred. The chief of staff of a formation in the field could record his disagreement with his commander in writing and appeal to the next higher command. For this reason Prussian victories were often credited to the chief of staff rather than the nominal commander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmuth-von-moltke-the-elder">Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</a>, chief from 1857, each year trained the twelve best graduates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-war-academy">Kriegsakademie</a> himself through war games, map exercises and staff rides, and called them the nervous system of the army. Against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he issued brief directives and left subordinate staffs to work out the details. After unification in 1871 it became the Imperial German General Staff, with officers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In August 1914 most of its officers joined the field armies, and the rest formed the General Staff of the Field Army within the Supreme Army Command.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> ordered the Great General Staff dissolved, but Hans von Seeckt disguised it as the Truppenamt and kept training staff officers in the military districts, and the former chief of staff <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> was elected Reichspräsident in 1925. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its influence declined, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>'s Luftwaffe gained weight and Hitler used the Armed Forces High Command as a rival planning staff. The Bundeswehr opened a new Army Academy in 1957.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “German General Staff” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prussian <b>General Staff</b>, raised to pre-eminence under Helmuth von Moltke the Elder, made strategic planning a professional discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
